            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ last index of x in new array is geq minus 1 
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ new array does not contain x 
        }
    }
}
```

When you run the unit test, the framework generates several test case behaviors for each of the elements in the input list. The framework makes sure the ArrayList's `Remove` method works in all its states. The code contract in the unit test (Assertion using Debug.Assert) checks that if an element is removed, the index of that element in the ArrayList should be greater than or equal to -1 and the latest index should also be greater than or equal to -1. Furthermore, it checks that the `Contains` method should return false for elements which were removed from the list.

For an element which was not in the ArrayList (the PexAssumeUnderTest assumption failed), the state of the Count property after remove does not affect the outcome of the tests. We are assuming `true` in the assumption that all valid test scenarios will be tested. This is done in order to reach the code coverage that is required.

The framework then checks if these conditions hold true for all the generated test cases.

#### Step 4: Add Assertions to Unit Test

We can add Debug Assertions to validate the outcome from above assertions:

```csharp
public void PUT_RemoveContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            arrList.Remove(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= -1, "Index of x in new array is geq minus 1");
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= -1);
--------------------	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= -1);
----------------------------------------	    // i.e., either it's -1 indicating x was not in arrList originally or it's an index >=0
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == -1 || New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0);
------------------------------------------------------------